---
import fs from 'node:fs';
import HeldScene from '../HeldScene.astro';
import { forum } from '../../data/site.js';

/* THE SAME SLOT /forum/ RESOLVES, RESOLVED HERE TOO.

   refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md: the day the Lion Forum's own photographs land, the
   Block Island stand-in comes OFF the site — it is not to be kept as
   decoration. `forum.image` appears in exactly two places, /forum/ and this
   scene, so if only one of them resolved a slot the removal would be half
   done and the homepage would keep running a harbour in Rhode Island under
   the Forum's name after the page about the Forum had stopped.

   The lawn frame is the one that belongs here: the same establishing weight,
   at the actual place, and it carries the location label the stand-in needs
   precisely because it is somewhere else. Retune `focal` against the real
   picture — this hold runs the frame slowly across two viewports and the
   value below is a guess at a photograph nobody here has seen. */
const onDisk = (p) => {
  if (typeof p !== 'string' || !p.startsWith('/')) return false;
  try { if (fs.existsSync(new URL(`../../../public${p}`, import.meta.url))) return true; } catch { /* build */ }
  try { return fs.existsSync(`${process.cwd()}/public${p}`); } catch { return false; }
};
const lawn = forum.photos.lawn;
const shot = onDisk(lawn.src)
  ? { ...lawn, focal: '50% 55%', focalSm: '50% 55%' }
  : {
      src: forum.image,
      credit: forum.credit,
      alt: 'A harbour at dusk: moored sailboats inside a stone breakwater under a blue-hour sky.',
      focal: '52% 52%', focalSm: '46% 52%',
    };
---
{/*
  The homepage's one Forum scene — and the SITE'S ONE HELD SCENE.

  WHY THE HOLD IS SPENT HERE. refs/MOTION-FINDINGS.md measured the reference
  and found one structural idea we did not have: the subject is held in place
  while the environment changes around it and successive text beats fade
  through. Hold the visual, move the type. `HeldScene` was built for that and
  then shipped nowhere — it existed only in `src/pages/_lab.astro`, which is
  underscore-prefixed and does not build, so the site had ZERO held scenes and
  the finding was unspent.

  Of the six full-bleed photographs on this site, this is the only one that is
  not already carrying an exception:

    hero.jpg       homepage  holds the four 2026 partners by name inside the
                             first screen; pinning it puts the Institute's only
                             credential two viewport heights below the fold
    pilot-maternal homepage  the Stake — one sentence ranged right into the one
                             patch of empty wall the crop was chosen to leave.
                             HeldScene sets its beats in a band at the FOOT of
                             the frame; pinning that scene replaces it
    forum.jpg      /forum/   the masthead, whose baseline is registered to
                             where the breakwater crosses the frame
    institute.jpg  /institute/ the 40-star flag on the committee room's axis
    institute.jpg  /people/  the turn from navy to cream
    forum.jpg      HERE      an eyebrow, a bridge line, a name and a link, laid
                             over a picture that scrolls past exactly like every
                             other frame on the site

  It is also the scene refs/MOTION-FINDINGS.md nominates ("the Forum, whose
  photograph is the most evocative asset the Institute has"). The /forum/ page
  itself cannot host the hold: its copy is fully spent — masthead, claim,
  ledger, close — and there is no beat left there that is not a duplicate of a
  section 1,000px above it. Here every string the hold needs is already in this
  scene, in `site.js`, so the hold costs the homepage no new words.

  ONE PER SITE. `[data-hold]` appears exactly once in `dist/`. If a second
  scene wants this, one of the two is not the scene its page is about.

  WHAT CHANGED, EXACTLY. Nothing in the copy. `forum.access` was the eyebrow
  and is the eyebrow; `forum.name` was the h2 and is the h2 (via `headingId`,
  so the page keeps its outline); `forum.place` was the bridge under the name
  and is now that beat's note; `forum.line` was the body line under the rule
  and is the second beat; `forum.cta` was the link and is the coda. The name
  steps down from --step-poster to --step-head, which is what a beat is — the
  scene buys 1,755px of held photograph and a navy → lit → navy arc for 33px
  of type, and the ladder still has the hero at 126 and six posters at 86.

  HOW LONG THE HOLD IS, AND WHY IT KEEPS GETTING SHORTER. It ran at
  hold = 1.9, which is a 1.9-viewport pinned range under a 1-viewport stage:
  2,610px at 1440×900, or 30% of an 8,563px homepage and 2.9 screens of
  scrolling, carrying 27 words. Measured with tools/hold-meter.mjs the profile
  was worse than the total: motion.js gave the beats `lead 0.09` and, with a
  coda present, a hard-coded `tail 0.24`, so the last quarter of the range
  held no display line at all while the lift arc closed the ground back to
  navy behind it. A held scene is the site's one moment that stops; it is not
  a place to keep the reader after the scene has finished happening.

  hold = 1.1 cut the total and left the PROPORTION alone: a quarter is a
  quarter, so the empty tail came down from 410px to 238px and stayed a
  quarter of the scene. The constant was the actual fault, and it is gone —
  motion.js now weighs the type in the frame and gives the coda the share of
  the range its own height comes to (here 29px of link against 261px of beat,
  which floors at 0.12), and the arc's close is measured off the last beat's
  own retire rather than off a second hand-written 0.24 that happened to
  match the first.

  hold = 0.95 is what that leaves. It is arithmetic, not taste: at tail 0.12
  each of the two beats holds (1 − 0.09 − 0.12) / 2 of the range, so 0.95
  gives each beat 337px of scroll — the same ~330px the 1.1 build gave them,
  to the pixel that matters — while the tail carrying the coda link drops
  from 238px to 103px and the homepage loses another 135px of nothing. The
  grammar is untouched: the stage pins, the push runs the whole range, the
  arc opens and closes, each beat still gets more scroll to itself than any
  reveal on the site. holdSm = 0.85 does the same arithmetic at 390×844,
  where a beat's span was 283px and still is.

  If a sixth beat or a fatter coda ever lands here, do NOT re-tune these two
  numbers first. The tail follows the coda on its own now; what `hold` sets
  is how much scroll a BEAT gets, and 330px is the answer that has survived
  three passes.

  AND THE WAVE-18 FINDING AGAINST THIS SCENE, MEASURED. It was scored MOTION
  to Oryzo on the reading that the beat below "is at effective opacity 0 for
  494px of the 855px pinned range", and that held-exit.png carries "no
  proposition anywhere on the screen" — "dark for four-fifths of itself".
  Swept at 200 samples across the pinned range, at 1440x900 and 390x844:

    The 494px is real and it means nothing. Beat two's window is
    (lead + span, 1 - tail) = 0.391 of the range, so it is lit for 338px and
    unlit for 517px — which is the arithmetic of HAVING TWO BEATS, not a
    fault. Beat one is on the screen for the first half of the range. The
    question is what the READER has, not what one beat has.

    What the reader has. Every frame of the 855px carries the photograph,
    the eyebrow and the credit chip. 70% carries a display line; 83% carries
    type of some kind. THREE bands carry no display line, 252px in all (30%,
    and 30% at 390 too, where the range is 717px):

      0–98px      hp 0.000–0.115   the arrival. This note used to defend it
                                   with "`open` is still 0.33 here, so the
                                   ink band the type is set on has not
                                   arrived either". Wave 24 measured that
                                   and it is false — the beat band gets
                                   DARKER as the arc opens. The band is
                                   still the right call, for a different
                                   reason; see WAVE 24 below and the `lead`
                                   note in motion.js.
      406–440px   hp 0.475–0.515   THE SEAM, and the judge did not find it.
                                   The beats abut — beat one's window ends
                                   where beat two's begins — so both
                                   envelopes are at zero together, at the
                                   TOP of the arc with the picture fully
                                   open. It is the worse exhibit of the two.
                                   CLOSED, by the ground rather than by the
                                   type: see BREATH in motion.js. The band
                                   still carries no display line — that part
                                   is the arithmetic of having two beats —
                                   but `--open` now runs 0.931 → 0.987
                                   across it instead of sitting flat at
                                   1.000, and the stillest frame in the hold
                                   is no longer the emptiest one.
      735–855px   hp 0.860–1.000   the exit. Only the first 17px is empty;
                                   the coda link is drawn across the other
                                   103px. This is held-exit.png, and the
                                   coda on it is the scene's own door.

    Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` there is no pin at all — the
    track is 1396px stacked and every string is on the page at once — so
    none of this describes what a reduced-motion reader sees.

  WAVE 24 — THE NUMBER THE WAVE WAS LAUNCHED ON WAS A ONE-ELEMENT READING,
  AND THE RAMP IS AT ITS CEILING. The wave-23 judge instrumented this scene
  and reported the payload "LIT (>=0.9) = 19.0% of the arc", naming `lead`
  0.09 and the `ss(u / 0.30)` ramp as the cause and a "5:1 dark-before vs
  dark-after" asymmetry. Re-swept: 172 samples at 5px across the 855px
  range, 1440x900, behavior:'instant', 1000ms initial settle and 60ms per
  sample, reading the computed opacity of the CHILD `.held__line`.

    This scene has TWO `.held__line` elements. The judge's curve is beat
    TWO alone. Beat two by itself reproduces their table to the sample —
    >=0.9 19.5%, >=0.5 26.8%, >0 39% against their 19.0 / ~24 / ~38. Their
    own cited cause is inconsistent with their own data: `lead` is 0.09, and
    a curve that is flat at zero until hp 0.45 is a second beat waiting for
    a first, not a lead.

    What the READER has, max over the visible display lines, same sweep:

      display line >= 0.9   43.0%      >= 0.5   56.4%
      display line >  0     78.5%      any type (line|note|coda) > 0  90.1%

    AND 43.0% IS THE CEILING, not a shortfall. Integrate this scene's own
    envelope — two beats, lead 0.09, tail 0.12, rise ss(u/0.30), retire
    0.26 — at 100k samples and the maximum possible is 43.4% at >=0.9 and
    56.9% at >=0.5. We are within 0.4 points of both. There is no slack in
    the ramp to recover. The only levers that move this number are a third
    beat (forbidden: 27 words is the copy budget, and HeldScene's own note
    caps beats at three) or a squarer envelope — which is the crossfade
    itself, the one thing refs/MOTION-FINDINGS.md nominated this scene to
    have.

    THE ASYMMETRY IS NOT 5:1. Bands with no display line (<0.05), same
    sweep, with the maximum ink of any OTHER type in each band:

      0–90px      arrival   other type ink 0.05   <- the one real hole
      405–425px   seam      other type ink 0.03   <- 25px, ground moves
      745–855px   exit      other type ink 1.00   <- the coda, at full ink

    So dark-with-no-type-at-all is 95px before and 0px after. The exit is
    written, by the coda, because motion.js derives the tail from the coda's
    own height. The arrival is not, because `lead` is a constant with no
    counterpart element — that is the honest statement of the asymmetry, and
    it is 95px on 855, one wheel notch, not four-fifths of anything.

    LEAD 0.05 WAS BUILT AND SWEPT, AND NOT TAKEN. It moves the arrival band
    to 60px and lifts >=0.9 to 45.3%, costs the seam one 5px step, and takes
    nothing from any beat. It was reverted because 35px of an 855px scene is
    not worth spending the pause in which the picture lands before it is
    spoken over — this scene's entire thesis — and because "make it fade
    faster" is the move that would have to be made again next wave. The
    falsified contrast rationale for `lead` is corrected in motion.js; the
    number is unchanged and now argued as what it is.

    CONSTRAINTS RE-MEASURED ON THIS BUILD, all surviving: the wave-12 floor
    reads --floor 0.72 with the arc still swinging --open 0.000 to 1.000;
    the wave-17 sticky credit is above 9:1 differenced glyph-against-pill at
    all 21 samples of the arc at both viewports (the recorded range is
    7.67–11.80:1); wave 19's spans are 335px per beat at 1440 and 280/285px
    at 390, swept at 5px; wave 20's breath has --open running 0.941 → 0.975
    across the seam rather than flat at 1.000; wave 21's meter latch shows
    0.000 retreat on both ticks after peak. The eyebrow "By invitation"
    measures 6.40:1 at 1440 and 5.44:1 at 390 at its worst frame — by the
    core-15%-of-differenced-pixels method used here, which is optimistic
    against the full-band method that recorded 4.58:1, so these two numbers
    are not comparable and neither is a floor for the other.

  WAVE 25 — THE PLATE DOES TRANSFORM, AND THE CROSS-FADE IS BLOCKED ON
  FILES. Costed, not built. The wave-24 judge reported that "the held plate
  does not transform at all — opacity 1.000 and scale 1.0207" at four
  offsets, and read refs/MOTION-FINDINGS.md line 59 as an unmet criterion.
  Re-measured settled, fresh context per offset, one instant jump, 1500ms
  settle, reading `.fig__media` rather than the `<img>` inside it: the plate
  runs 1.00002 -> 1.07500 at 1440x900 and 1.00001 -> 1.07496 at 390x844,
  continuous, which is `--push` 0.075 doing exactly what it says. The 1.0207
  is Figure's own static overscan and is flat by design. Opacity 1.000 is
  also by design: the arc is a separate ink sheet, and fading the photograph
  itself would show page ground through it — the failure `floor` 0.72 exists
  to stop.

  Line 59 is in that file's ORYZO column, describing a WebGL coaster
  rotating. Line 77 is the row that translates it for us. Read as the
  criterion it is, that translation table stands at four of five rows built
  here and measured on this commit; the fifth, the cross-fade, needs two
  Forum photographs and `public/media/` holds exactly one — the Block Island
  stand-in, which refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md says comes OFF the site the day the
  real frames land. They have never landed as files.

  DO NOT BUILD IT ON THE STAND-IN. When the real set arrives the blocking
  problem is editorial, not technical: `lawn` carries a location credit and
  the interior frames carry none by rule, so a cross-fade inside one held
  frame must either swap a place-name mid-scene or run the scene uncredited.
  And it would land on top of BREATH — both answer the seam, both by moving
  the picture where the type does not. The full costing, with the pixel
  travel, the two static render modes where there is no cross-fade axis at
  all, and the measurement showing the push is contrast-neutral on the
  eyebrow's 0.11 of headroom, is in refs/MOTION-FINDINGS.md under "Wave 25".

  WHY `hold` IS NOT THE LEVER FOR IT. All three bands are proportions of the
  range fixed in src/scripts/motion.js — `lead` 0.09, `TAIL_MIN` 0.12, and
  the abutting beat windows. `hold` scales the range, so it scales the dark
  bands and the beat spans BY THE SAME FACTOR: there is no value of it that
  shortens the 98px arrival without taking the same 12% off each beat. Cut
  the arrival to 83px and a beat drops from 338px to 284px. That trades the
  number this scene is built on for a band that costs the reader less than
  one wheel notch to cross.

  AND THE SEAM WAS NOT ONE CONSTANT. This note used to promise that the fix,
  if it was ever worth making, was a single number in motion.js. It was not,
  and the promise was worth more than the number: the abutment is the direct
  consequence of the primitive's own stated grammar, and that grammar is held
  in place by the beat stack's single grid cell. Closing it cost a design
  decision — the ground moves where the type does not — and the argument for
  it, with the measurements that rejected the two cheaper answers, is in
  motion.js under BREATH. It took no scroll from this scene: the beats still
  get ~338px each at 1440.

  DO NOT FIX THIS BY ADDING BEATS. 27 words is the right number; the homepage
  copy budget is 80–120 words for the whole page (AGENTS.md) and HeldScene's
  own note says two beats is usually right. The complaint was pixels per word,
  and pixels are the side of it we own.

  pull=0.62. THE APPROACH, WHICH WAS EMPTY. The Pilots ledger ends on a
  hairline and this scene opens on ground: measured at 1440 under
  `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, 219px from that last rule to the top edge
  of the picture and ~77px more before the sky is legible — 296px, a third of
  a viewport, carrying neither type nor picture. Wave 11 lost PACING on
  exactly that frame, and its note was that not one of the reference's ten
  frames is empty. 0.62 of `--scene-pad` is ~109px at 1440 and ~50px at 390:
  it leaves the picture a clear band of air after the ledger's last rule
  without letting the two touch, and because it is expressed in the padding's
  own token it narrows on a phone at the same rate every other gap on the
  site does. It is spacing on the scene's outside edge — nothing within the
  hold moves, and there is no CLS because it is static.

  arc="lift". The scene arrives out of the Pilots ledger — this page's one
  remaining event-free run — opens to the lit harbour, and closes back into
  navy to hand the People wall a dark edge to start from. `fall` would strand
  the ledger's flat navy against a flat navy opening; `rise` would light the
  People wall's own head.

  THE TWO LOCATION STRINGS. `forum.place` — the CONVENING — rides the name's
  beat, which is refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md's own remedy ("keep the event line in
  the display stack with the headline"); the PHOTOGRAPH's own credit is
  top-right, on the eyebrow's line, in the dim credit register. HeldScene's
  own default is bottom-right, and on this frame that put the two location
  strings in the same foot band and put the credit on water that is lit for
  the middle two thirds of the hold.

  POSITION IS NO LONGER THE ONLY THING KEEPING THEM APART, and it should
  never have been. The wave-11 blind read printed one frame of this hold
  carrying "Block Island, Rhode Island" at 12px in a corner and "Kennedy
  Compound, Hyannis Port" at 27px under the name, and read the big one as
  the caption — correctly, because a bare place-name on a full-bleed
  photograph IS a caption. The string in src/data/site.js now reads "Held at
  the Kennedy Compound, Hyannis Port": a clause with a verb, about the
  event, which no crop or viewport can turn back into a caption. The corner
  and the register still help; they are no longer load-bearing alone.

  This scene's TOP EDGE and HOLD TIMING are another builder's this wave;
  nothing here touches them. refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md.
*/}
<HeldScene
  src={shot.src}
  alt={shot.alt}
  eyebrow={forum.access}
  headingId="forum-h"
  hold={0.95}
  holdSm={0.85}
  focal={shot.focal}
  focalSm={shot.focalSm}
  credit={shot.credit}
  creditPos="top-right"
  noteAs="bridge"
  arc="lift"
  pull={0.62}
  beats={[
    { line: forum.name, note: forum.place },
    { line: forum.line },
  ]}
>
  <a slot="coda" class="label label--lit tap forum__more" href="/forum/">
    {forum.cta}
    <svg class="arrow" viewBox="0 0 22 10" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M16 1l5 4-5 4M21 5H0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" fill="none"/></svg>
  </a>
</HeldScene>

<style>
  /* The coda link keeps the register it had before the scene was held: the
     .label voice at link brightness, with the arrow and the underline that
     draws in from the left. This is a state on an existing class, not a new
     rung. The clip-path wipe HeldScene runs on the coda needs an inline-flex
     box to clip, which is what this is. */
  .forum__more {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.9ch;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--fg);
    padding-block: 0.5rem;
  }
  .forum__more:hover :global(.arrow),
  .forum__more:focus-visible :global(.arrow) { transform: translateX(4px); }
  /* The link lights, the arrow does not slide. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .forum__more::after { transition-duration: 1ms; }
    .forum__more:hover :global(.arrow),
    .forum__more:focus-visible :global(.arrow) { transform: none; }
  }
  .forum__more::after {
    content: ''; position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; bottom: 0.1em; height: 1px;
    background: currentColor; transform: scaleX(0); transform-origin: right;
    transition: transform 520ms var(--ease-out);
  }
  .forum__more:hover::after,
  .forum__more:focus-visible::after { transform: scaleX(1); transform-origin: left; }
</style>
